# Operator policy for {name}, a Pollen Robotics Microduck. The gateway reads
# this at start. Values are quoted: the tier bindings contain '|', which a
# shell would otherwise treat as a pipeline when this file is sourced.
#
# Two independent gates, and the split is the point:
#   ROBOT_MD_TOOL_ALLOWLIST  which tools exist at all. Anything absent is a
#                            signed DENY before any driver code runs.
#   ROBOT_MD_TOOL_MIN_TIER   which BEARER tiers may invoke each tool. Keys off
#                            the tool name (operator-controlled), not the
#                            envelope's self-declared `scope` (caller-controlled).
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHY THERE IS NO `duck.move` IN THIS LIST, and why that is the honest file.
#
# A duck is not a car. On the rc-car archetype the gateway OWNS the wheels: the
# rc-car actuator holds the PWM channels, the envelope budget and the deadman,
# so `drive.set` on this rail is the only path to motion and gating it gates
# the vehicle. On a Microduck, robotd owns the motor bus exclusively and runs
# its own 50 Hz loop, its own fall detection and its own 500 ms twist deadman.
# There is no `microduck` actuator for this gateway (`castor up` resolves
# `noop` and says so), so a `duck.move` written here would be accepted, signed,
# receipted and delivered to nothing.
#
# That is trap 7 of the Microduck review wearing a third costume: a command
# that succeeds without moving anything. So it is not in the allowlist. Motion
# for this robot travels `castor run` -> MicroduckDriver -> {name}-duckbridge
# -> robotd, which is a path where a refusal is a refusal and a move is a move.
#
# What IS on this rail is real: the signed identity, the capability surface,
# the read tier, and the stop. `duck.stop` is here because a stop must exist on
# every path that exists at all; it reaches the runtime's /api/stop, which
# reaches the driver, which reaches robotd.
ROBOT_MD_TOOL_ALLOWLIST="duck.stop,status.report"
#
# `duck.stop` is pinned at `read`, the lowest tier this gateway can express.
# A stop that a privilege check can refuse is not a stop, and there is no
# failure mode in which declining to halt a walking robot is the safer outcome.
ROBOT_MD_TOOL_MIN_TIER="duck.stop:read|actuate|commission,status.report:read|actuate|commission"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE VELOCITY ENVELOPE. These are read by the runtime, not the gateway, and
# they are here because this is the file an operator edits and expects to be
# read. They are the numbers `MicroduckDriver` scales full stick deflection to.
#
# 0.2 m/s is OpenCastor's default and it is FIVE TIMES SLOWER than the gamepad
# Pollen ships, which drives the same duck at 0.3 m/s (padd --max-linear). A
# duck that "barely moves" under OpenCastor and walks briskly under the pad is
# not a broken duck; it is this number. Raise it deliberately, up to 0.3, and
# no further without reading Pollen's own limits.
OPENCASTOR_DUCK_MAX_VX=0.2
OPENCASTOR_DUCK_MAX_VY=0.1
OPENCASTOR_DUCK_MAX_VYAW=1.0
#
# Three deadmen sit under this robot and they are deliberately different
# numbers, each covering the failure the one below it cannot see:
#
#   1.5 s   MicroduckDriver.command_ttl_s   the last move() expires; the intent
#                                           loop stops re-sending a twist
#                                           nobody renewed.
#   700 ms  {name}-duckbridge --deadman     the CLIENT vanished (a pocket, a
#                                           dropped Wi-Fi, a killed app). The
#                                           bridge sends one robot.stop.
#   500 ms  robotd's own twist deadman      THIS PROCESS vanished. Nothing in
#                                           OpenCastor can raise, lower or
#                                           disable it, which is why it is the
#                                           real backstop and the other two are
#                                           conveniences above it.
#
# Only the first two are ours. Changing either does not change the third.
